※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ A truth value is one byte in memory, which is what the layout says it is.
※ Reading or writing one any wider would reach past it into whatever is laid
※ out beside it -- and what is laid out beside it is another variable.  The two
※ guards here are what such a read or write would disturb.

@[visible]
let before: mut u8 = 5u8

let ready: bool = true

@[visible]
let seen: mut bool = false

@[visible]
let after: mut u8 = 6u8

@[startup]
fn main() → u8:
    seen ← ready
    ※ Nothing here can look at the guards yet -- there is no way to compare --
    ※ so what this asserts is that the program runs to its end and exits
    ※ cleanly; the widths themselves are asserted in tests/compiler.
    0u8
